# Part Three — The Examiner's Record of Topic 1

## Every question, 2016–2026

@py topic_questions 1

## What the record reveals

- **Sixteen full questions and one short note in eleven papers** — the second-heaviest topic, after Gender and Development.
- **The discipline half repeats relentlessly.** Women's vs Gender Studies (2017, 2018, 2019, 2023), status in Pakistan (2016, 2018, 2019, 2023), autonomy vs integration (2016, 2017, 2021, 2023) and the multidisciplinary nature (2022, 2023). The same four families, recombined.
- **The construction half is getting harder.** From "what are the theories?" (2016) to "deconstruct gender" (2020), "natural or social?" (2021), "is sex a construct?" (2024) and queer theory applied to Pakistani law (2026).
- **Near repeats are common.** 2016 Q2 and 2023 Q2 are almost identical (status plus autonomy–integration). Expect a third appearance.
- **A three-year silence.** No question on the discipline half since 2023 — the longest gap in the record. It is due.

## Five likely questions

1. "Trace the historical development of social constructionism in the study of gender. How has it changed our understanding of 'woman'?" *(Chapter 6 — the syllabus line never asked by name.)*
2. "Critically evaluate the achievements and challenges of Gender Studies as an academic discipline in Pakistan." *(Chapter 5, with Chapter 4.)*
3. "Is biology destiny? Discuss the nature versus culture debate with reference to gender roles in Pakistani society." *(Chapters 9–10.)*
4. "Explain Connell's concept of hegemonic masculinity. How does it help explain gender inequality in Pakistan?" *(Chapter 9.)*
5. "Queer theory argues that even 'sex' is socially constructed. Critically examine this claim." *(Chapter 8.)*

These are not solved here; the Prediction Papers include answers to predicted questions. Plan each as an answer map for practice.
